## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests a foundational dynamic programming pattern—selecting k non-adjacent elements with maximum sum. It assesses understanding of state definition, transition logic, and reconstruction, making it a classic beginner DP problem.

## Key Concepts
- **State definition**: dp[i][j] represents the optimal sum for the first i elements with exactly j selections
- **Transition logic**: Compare skipping the current element vs. taking it (with the appropriate previous state)
- **Backtracking**: Reconstruct chosen indices by comparing states during backtracking
- **Feasibility constraint**: Maximum selections possible in i elements is floor((i+1)/2)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumNonAdjacent(A, m) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(m + 1).fill(NEG));

  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let j = 1; j <= Math.min(m, Math.floor((i + 1) / 2)); j++) {
      const take = A[i - 1] + (i >= 2 ? dp[i - 2][j - 1] : (j - 1 === 0 ? 0 : NEG));
      const skip = dp[i - 1][j];
      dp[i][j] = Math.max(skip, take);
    }
  }

  const result = dp[n][m];
  const indices = [];
  let i = n, j = m;

  while (i > 0 && j > 0) {
    const take = A[i - 1] + (i >= 2 ? dp[i - 2][j - 1] : (j - 1 === 0 ? 0 : NEG));
    if (dp[i][j] === take) {
      indices.push(i - 1);
      j--;
      i -= 2;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }

  return { sum: result, indices: indices.reverse() };
}
```

**Time Complexity:** O(n × m)
**Space Complexity:** O(n × m), or O(m) with optimization.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests time-expanded DP on grids for path counts with constraints.

## Key Concepts
- DP on (i,j,t) layers; space optimization; modulo arithmetic.
- Handling walls and boundary checks.

## Code Example
```javascript
function countPaths(grid, k) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const MOD = 1_000_000_007;
  if (grid[0][0] === 1 || grid[n-1][m-1] === 1) return 0;
  let prev = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(m).fill(0));
  prev[0][0] = 1;
  const dirs = [[1,0],[-1,0],[0,1],[0,-1]];
  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    const cur = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(m).fill(0));
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      if (grid[i][j] === 1) continue;
      for (const [dx,dy] of dirs) {
        const x = i + dx, y = j + dy;
        if (x>=0 && x<n && y>=0 && y<m && grid[x][y]===0) {
          cur[i][j] = (cur[i][j] + prev[x][y]) % MOD;
        }
      }
    }
    prev = cur;
  }
  return prev[n-1][m-1];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP design for selecting multiple disjoint subarrays with reconstruction. Requires prefix sums, state definition, and backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- DP over positions and count of subarrays
- Prefix sums for subarray sums
- Reconstructing intervals with predecessor pointers
- Time/space trade-offs (O(nk) vs optimizations)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length; const P = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) P[i] = P[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const from = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>new Array(k+1).fill(null));
  for (let i=0;i<=n;i++) dp[i][0] = 0;
  for (let t=1;t<=k;t++){
    let best = -Infinity;
    let bestIdx = -1;
    for (let i=1;i<=n;i++){
      if (dp[i-1][t-1] - P[i-1] > best){ best = dp[i-1][t-1] - P[i-1]; bestIdx = i-1; }
      const take = P[i] + best;
      if (take > dp[i-1][t]){ dp[i][t] = take; from[i][t] = [bestIdx+1, i]; }
      else { dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t]; }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction (omitted for brevity in this snippet)
  return {maxSum: dp[n][k], intervals: []};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle exactly k vs at most k subarrays?
- Can you reduce space to O(n) or O(k)?
- How would you reconstruct intervals efficiently without extra passes?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem combines two key challenges: optimizing over two sequences simultaneously and selecting exactly k non-overlapping intervals. It tests your ability to formulate dynamic programming solutions for constrained partitioning problems and handle solution reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- 2D prefix sums for O(1) range sum queries
- Non-overlapping interval DP with exact count constraint
- Path reconstruction via backpointers
- Trade-off between intervals via knapsack-like combining

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxIntervalValue(A, B, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const PA = [0], PB = [0];
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    PA[i] = PA[i-1] + A[i-1];
    PB[i] = PB[i-1] + B[i-1];
  }
  const dp = Array(n+1).fill(null).map(() => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array(n+1).fill(null).map(() => Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let r = 1; r <= n; r++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      for (let l = 1; l <= r; l++) {
        const val = (PA[r] - PA[l-1]) * (PB[r] - PB[l-1]);
        const candidate = dp[l-1][t-1] + val;
        if (candidate > dp[r][t]) {
          dp[r][t] = candidate;
          prev[r][t] = { l, r: l-1 };
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates DP on selecting exact number of non-overlapping subarrays with reconstruction, common in profit/segmentation tasks.

## Key Concepts
- Non-overlapping subarrays
- Exact k selections
- Prefix sums for efficient range sums
- Backpointers for reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k){
  const n = A.length; const pre = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) pre[i] = pre[i-1]+A[i-1];
  const neg inf = -1e18;
  let dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> new Array(k+1).fill(neg_inf));
  let best = new Array(k+1).fill(neg_inf);
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let t=1;t<=k;t++) best[t] = neg_inf;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      best[t] = Math.max(best[t], dp[i-1][t-1] - pre[i-1]);
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i-1][t], pre[i] + best[t]);
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

